-    ["/methodology/", "How Stock Value Lens works", "Understand annual EPS, cash flow, growth, price adjustments, and the valuation reference.", `<h1>Methodology</h1><p>Compare historical weekly prices with annual reported fundamentals. All features are free.</p><h2>Annual reported facts</h2><p>Basic and diluted EPS come from supported SEC Company Facts tags in 10-K and 10-K/A filings; EPS is not synthesized. The importer checks annual durations, selects matching periods, deduplicates restated observations, and applies later standard-taxonomy stock-split facts to per-share values and diluted shares. Filing accession, tag, unit, and filing date are retained when available. Coverage varies by issuer and taxonomy.</p><h2>Free cash flow per share</h2><p>Simple FCF is operating cash flow minus cash capital expenditure. FCF/share divides this by annual diluted weighted-average shares when matching facts are available. It is a derived measure, not a standardized GAAP figure. Banks, REITs, and other businesses may have unavailable or economically unsuitable FCF measures.</p><h2>P/E ratio history</h2><p>Every stored weekly close divided by the most recent annual basic or diluted EPS by fiscal-period end, carried forward without interpolation. Adjusted-close ratios are approximate and retrospective, not TTM or forward P/E. <a href="/guides/pe-ratio-history/">Read P/E history rules, gaps and reference lines.</a></p><h2>Growth and the orange reference line</h2><p>CAGR uses the first and last positive annual observations within the selected chart window. Elapsed years use exact fiscal-end dates divided by 365.2425 days.</p><p>CAGR = (last value ÷ first value)<sup>1 / years</sup> − 1.</p><p>Reference multiple = min(60, 15 × ((1 + CAGR) ÷ 1.10)<sup>years × 0.6</sup>), with CAGR expressed as a decimal. Multiply each positive per-share metric by this multiple. For FCF/share, the multiple is applied to FCF rather than earnings. A custom positive multiple is also capped at 60.</p><p>Missing or non-positive observations create gaps in the reference line. The orange line is a formula reference, not an intrinsic-value estimate or price target. It uses retrospective information and is not a point-in-time investment backtest.</p><h2>Prices, ranges, and dividends</h2><p>Weekly data retains the last available row in each week. Prefer validated split-only closes when supplied; otherwise the chart uses labeled Stooq adjusted closes as an approximation. The chart ends at the latest available price date. Company tables retain newer annual filings independently.</p><p><a href="/guides/fiscal-year-dividend-yield/">Fiscal-year dividend yield</a> uses an annual reported dividend and a historical year-end close. It is off by default on the chart.</p><p><a href="/sources/">Source dates and limitations</a> · <a href="/about/">Author and corrections</a></p>`],
+    ["/methodology/", "How Stock Value Lens works", "A plain-language guide to EPS, cash flow, P/E, CAGR and the formula’s 10% growth benchmark and 0.6 factor.", `<h1>Methodology</h1><p>Stock Value Lens puts past stock prices beside a company's yearly earnings, cash flow and dividends. This page explains where the numbers come from and how the chart's formula works. All features are free.</p><h2>Earnings per share (EPS)</h2><p>EPS means earnings per share: the company's profit for each share. Basic EPS uses its average number of ordinary shares. Diluted EPS also allows for potential extra shares, such as those from employee stock options. You can choose either measure in the chart.</p><p>We use annual figures reported to the US Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC), through its Company Facts data service. These come from company annual reports (10-K) and corrections to those reports (10-K/A). We do not invent EPS when a reported figure is missing.</p><p>We match figures to the same financial year, avoid counting repeated reports twice, and use updated figures when the stored reports include corrections. Where supported stock-split records are available, we adjust earlier per-share figures and share counts so they can be compared on the newer share basis. For example, a two-for-one split doubles the number of shares and halves EPS per share.</p><p>Source links let you check the original filing. We keep the filing date, filing number, reported item and unit when available. Some companies report items in formats we cannot read, so coverage varies.</p><h2>Free cash flow per share (FCF/share)</h2><p>Free cash flow is a simple way to compare cash coming from the business with cash spent on long-term assets, such as equipment and buildings.</p><p class="worked-formula">Free cash flow = cash from operations − cash spending on long-term assets<br>FCF/share = free cash flow ÷ average diluted share count for the same financial year</p><p>The average share count takes account of changes during the year. We calculate FCF/share only when the cash-flow figures and share count cover the same annual period. It is our calculation from reported inputs, not a standard accounting measure every company defines in the same way. It may be missing or unsuitable for banks, property trusts (REITs) and some other businesses.</p><h2>Price-to-earnings ratio (P/E)</h2><p>P/E compares a stock's price with its earnings per share. A price of $100 and annual EPS of $5 give a P/E of 20×: the price is 20 times those annual earnings.</p><p class="worked-formula">P/E = stock price ÷ annual EPS</p><p>For each stored weekly price, we use the latest annual EPS whose financial year has ended by that price date. We keep using it until the next financial year ends; we do not fill in imaginary earnings between annual figures. Missing, zero or negative EPS leaves a gap.</p><p>This uses yearly reported earnings, rather than earnings for the latest rolling 12 months (TTM) or an analyst's forecast of next year's earnings (forward P/E). Reports and corrections can arrive after the year ends, so the chart may use information that investors did not yet have on that date. Adjusted prices can also make the ratio approximate. <a href="/guides/pe-ratio-history/">Read the P/E history guide.</a></p><h2>Annual growth: what CAGR means</h2><p>CAGR stands for compound annual growth rate. It answers: what steady yearly growth rate would turn the starting value into the ending value? The actual yearly changes can be much less steady.</p><p>For example, a value rising from $2 to $2.42 in two years has a CAGR of 10%, because $2 × 1.10 × 1.10 = $2.42.</p><p>We use the first and last positive annual values inside your selected chart window. We measure the time between their actual financial year-end dates, using 365.2425 days per year. At least two positive annual values are needed. A zero or loss cannot serve as an endpoint, and losses between the endpoints are not directly used in this calculation.</p><p class="worked-formula">CAGR = (last value ÷ first value)<sup>1 / years</sup> − 1</p><p>In the formulas below, write CAGR as a decimal: 10% is 0.10 and 20% is 0.20. A very small starting value can create a very high growth rate. Changing the chart window can change the endpoints, CAGR and formula line.</p><h2>Why we use the orange formula line</h2><p>The orange line gives a simple, consistent way to compare past prices with earnings or cash flow per share. We start with a multiple of 15×, then adjust it using the selected period's growth compared with our 10% annual growth target. Longer periods affect the adjustment more, with a 0.6 factor to give the history only partial weight.</p><p>A multiple is simply a number used to multiply a per-share figure. For example, EPS of $5 × a multiple of 20 gives a formula reference of $100. In the FCF/share view, we multiply FCF/share instead of EPS.</p><p class="worked-formula">Reference multiple = min(60, 15 × ((1 + CAGR) ÷ 1.10)<sup>years × 0.6</sup>)<br>Formula value = positive annual EPS or FCF/share × reference multiple</p><p>The formula uses the same selected-window multiple for every positive annual value in that window. Missing, zero or negative values leave gaps. You can enter your own positive multiple instead, without the formula’s 60× cap. Leave the custom input blank or use Reset to return to the automatic formula. In the formula, min(60, …) means use the smaller of 60 and the calculated result.</p><h2>Why the 10% target?</h2><p>We use 10% as a fixed annual growth benchmark for the business measure you selected: EPS or FCF/share. It gives the formula a clear comparison point.</p><ul><li>At exactly 10% growth, (1 + CAGR) ÷ 1.10 equals 1, so the multiple stays at 15×.</li><li>Above 10%, the formula raises the multiple, up to the 60× cap.</li><li>Below 10%, the formula lowers the multiple below 15×.</li></ul><p>The 10% target is a choice for this formula. It is not a forecast or promise that the stock price, earnings or your investment will rise by 10%.</p><h2>Why multiply the years by 0.6?</h2><p>We give a long history of growth partial credit because past growth may not continue. The 0.6 factor reduces the number of years used in the exponent: 10 actual years become 6 in this part of the formula. This softens the effect of a long growth period on the multiple.</p><p>For growth above 10%, it produces a smaller increase than using all the years. For growth below 10%, it also softens the reduction, keeping the multiple closer to 15×. It does not reduce the final value by 40% or act as a cash-flow discount rate.</p><p>The exact 0.6 factor, 15× starting multiple and 60× cap are choices for this comparison tool. They are not company-specific estimates of risk or proven fair-value rules.</p><h2>A worked formula example</h2><p>Suppose EPS grew by 20% per year over exactly 10 years and the ending annual EPS was $5. These are made-up inputs to show the arithmetic.</p><p class="worked-formula">Years × 0.6 = 10 × 0.6 = 6<br>Multiple = 15 × (1.20 ÷ 1.10)<sup>6</sup> ≈ 25.28×<br>Formula value = $5 × 25.28 ≈ $126.41</p><p>Without the 0.6 factor, using an exponent of 10 would give about 35.81×. The factor softens the increase. The 60× cap does not apply in this example because 25.28 is below 60. Values above are rounded; the calculation uses full precision.</p><h2>How to read the result</h2><p>The orange line is a historical formula reference, not a price target or an estimate of what a company is truly worth. It does not separately forecast future earnings, debt or future investment needs. A stock below the line is not automatically cheap, and a stock above it is not automatically expensive. A 60× cap limits the output; it does not make that output reliable for every company.</p><p>The chart uses stored reports, including later corrections and supported split adjustments. It does not recreate exactly what investors knew at each date, so it cannot by itself test how a trading strategy would have performed.</p><h2>Prices, dates and dividends</h2><p>We keep the last available closing price in each week. Where a checked price series adjusted only for stock splits is available, we prefer it. Otherwise, we use Stooq adjusted closing prices and label them as approximations. Adjusted prices can reflect dividends as well as splits, so they may differ from the price actually traded on the date.</p><p>The chart ends at the latest stored price date. A company's full annual table can include financial reports newer than that date. These are historical snapshots, not live quotes. A company's financial year can also end on a different date from the calendar year.</p><p>Annual dividend yield compares the reported dividend per share for a financial year with a price near that year's end. It is not a forecast of next year's dividend or a yield based on today's price. The dividend overlay starts switched off. <a href="/guides/fiscal-year-dividend-yield/">Read the dividend-yield calculation.</a></p><p><a href="/sources/">Check data sources, dates and limitations</a> · <a href="/about/">About the author and reporting an error</a>. This site is for historical research and education, not investment advice.</p>`],
