+## ISSUE-014 — Formula assumptions and custom limits were unclear
+
+- **Date:** 2026-10-10
+- **Status:** Resolved
+- **Area:** Formula explanations and custom-multiple controls.
+- **Symptoms:** Visitor feedback treated Amazon's formula reference as a bullish valuation. The methodology did not explain the 10% benchmark or 0.6 factor in everyday language. A custom 100× input silently became 60×.
+- **Cause:** Technical documentation omitted the rationale for fixed formula choices, and the automatic formula's ceiling was also applied to custom values in both the input markup and calculation helper.
+- **Solution:** Explain the 10% business-metric growth benchmark, the 0.6 partial weighting and their limits with checked examples; define financial terms plainly. Label CAGR directly. Apply the 60× ceiling only to the automatic formula and allow positive finite custom values above it; update the guide, UI, fixtures and README consistently.
+- **Why this solution:** Separates the automatic comparison rule from the visitor's chosen assumption. The original Python documentation confirms partial credit for long histories. The 0.6 factor changes the exponent, not the final value by 40%, and also softens reductions below 10% growth; the wording must not imply a promised stock return or a calibrated risk model.
+- **Verification:** Two revised 100× reference cases and the new regression failed before the fix and pass afterward. The complete suite has 43 Node and 5 Python passes. Further build, browser and publication evidence is in VERIFICATION.md.
+- **Follow-up:** Keep the automatic cap and custom-input behavior distinct in future changes. A formula reference remains separate from a company-specific valuation.
+- **References:** `scripts/site.mjs`, `web/calculations.js`, `web/index.html`, `web/app.js`, `tests/calculations.test.js`, `tests/fixtures/README.md`; original `fastfunds/core.py::fair_value_multiple` duration-weight explanation.
+
